he way of someone who's waited seventeen years and can wait no longer. Nora Ellis felt the old panic rise: run, before it breaks."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Choice', 'text': "She found him at the shore, where it started. 'I choose,' she said, breathless, 'the thing that scares me. I choose you. I've always chosen you — I just took seventeen years to say it.'\n\nher overbearing mother left town with the gift wrap and the almosts. Nora Ellis felt nothing but pity — and then not even that, because her arms were full of present tense.\n\na vintage locket got a place of honor on the mantel — not as a relic, but as a reminder: that love lost can be love found, that home is a person, and that home was never a town at all."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise', 'text': 'home is a person — Nora Ellis wrote it in the new journal, the one for the next seventeen years. In Willow Creek, under the stars, with love: the whole story, and only just beginning.\n\nThey danced on the porch to the mixtape — no, to a new playlist, because the past is a place to visit, not to live. Nora Ellis had learned that at last.\n\nHe proposed with a vintage locket — fitting, her colleague Priya said, since it started everything. Nora Ellis said yes before he finished asking, because seventeen years is long enough to wait for a sentence.'}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue in Wildflowers', 'text': "Nora Ellis walks the shore most evenings, sometimes alone, sometimes with him, always with Willow Creek around her like a held breath. The sunsets haven't changed. She has — into someone who stays.\n\nhome is a person. In Willow Creek, in the wildflowers, in the salt air, in every sunset: the whole story, still beginning, never ending.\n\nher colleague Priya still diagnoses everything with pie. Her grandmother Rose's quilt is worn thin with love. her overbearing mother is a story they tell laughing, a ghost with no haunting left in it."}]}

